\description{
The generalized method of moments estimators of a dynamic panel with
unit effects: the difference estimator of Arellano and Bond (1991) and
the system estimator of Arellano and Bover (1995) and Blundell and Bond
(1998), with the instrument matrix built as Roodman (2009) describes
it. Andreatta et al. (2023, pp. 1372-1374, table 3) fit the logged rate
of waste crime in Italian provinces this way: one-step system GMM with
robust errors, time effects, the lagged rate and four enforcement and
organised-crime variables instrumented GMM-style by their second and
third lags, the AR(1) and AR(2) tests, the Hansen test, the Hansen test
of the differenced equations alone and the difference-in-Hansen test of
the level instruments, with no more instruments than provinces.
}
\details{
The model is `y_it = a_1 y_i,t-1 + ... + a_L y_i,t-L + x_it b + d_t + u_i
\itemize{
\item v_it\code{with}L = ylag\verb{. Periods are the distinct values of }time\verb{in order: lags count positions in that order, and a unit without a row in a period has a gap there. A row enters the levels when the unit has the}ylag\verb{ periods before it; the transformed equation is the first difference of two such rows in consecutive periods (}trans = "fd"\verb{), or the forward orthogonal deviation of a row from the mean of the unit's later rows, scaled by }sqrt(T / (T + 1))\verb{ and dated one period later (}trans = "fod"`, Arellano and Bover 1995, sec. 3; Roodman 2009, eq 21),
which loses no more than the last row of a unit with gaps.
}

GMM-style instruments (Roodman 2009, sec. 3.2) are made for the lagged
outcome (when \code{ylag > 0}, first) and for the columns of \code{x} named in
\code{gmm}: for the transformed equation dated \code{t}, the levels of the
variable at \code{t - l} for \code{l} from \code{lagmin} to \code{lagmax}, zero where the
unit has no row, one column per period and lag, or one per lag with
\code{collapse = TRUE}. For the level equation of system GMM, the difference
at \code{t - lagmin + 1} (one column per period, or one); the other lags are
redundant (Roodman 2009, sec. 3.4). Lags count from the variable: the
default \code{lagmin = 2} takes \verb{y_i,t-2} and earlier for the equation in
\code{t}, the standard choice for an endogenous variable, and \code{lagmin = 1}
treats a column as predetermined. IV-style instruments (one column each,
transformed in the transformed equation and in levels in the level
equation, Roodman 2009, eq 24) are the columns of \code{x} in \code{iv}, the
columns of \code{z}, the time dummies and, in system GMM, the constant
(which is in the level equation only). Columns of instruments that are
zero throughout are dropped; time dummies collinear with the other
regressors in the stacked equations are dropped, and named in
\code{dropped}.

The one-step weight is \verb{(sum_i Z_i' H_i Z_i)^-1}. With \code{hmat = "ab"}
(the default), \code{H_i} is what the errors' variance would be with
independent, homoskedastic idiosyncratic errors and no unit effects in
the level equation: \verb{M M'} for the transformed rows (2 on the diagonal
and -1 next to it for differences, the identity for deviations), \code{M}
between transformed and level rows and the identity for the levels
(Roodman 2009, eqs 22-23 and 26); \code{"dpd"} zeroes the blocks between the
equations, and \code{"id"} takes the identity throughout, as Blundell and
Bond (1998) did. The two-step weight is the inverse of \verb{sum_i Z_i' e_i e_i' Z_i} with the one-step residuals. A singular weight matrix is
replaced by its Moore-Penrose inverse, and the result says so. With
\code{robust = TRUE} (the default) the one-step variance is the robust
sandwich (Roodman 2009, eq 15) and the two-step variance is corrected as
Windmeijer (2005) shows (Roodman 2009, eq 18); with \code{robust = FALSE} the
one-step variance is the conventional \verb{s2 (X'Z A Z'X)^-1}, eq 14 with
\verb{Var(z e) = s2 Z'HZ}, \verb{A = (Z'HZ)^-1} and \code{s2} as for Sargan's statistic
